/**

@page swift_particle_new_solver Creating new solvers



# New solver kernels

If you want to equip your new solver with vectorisation facilities, you have to
invest some work to be able to exploit
@ref page_toolbox_particles_mesh_traversal "Peano's optimised memory storage schemes".


## Python API

Vectorisation can work if and only if the underlying memory over multiple
particles is continuous and therefore allows for coalescent memory access.
Likley, it nowadays can work for scattered memory, too, but that is more
work. You would then have to gather the particles before you invoke the
actual compute kernel. I discuss here only solutions where this gathering
is already done within the mesh. If you decide to gather yourself, you can,
after this is complete, follow the recipes below.

Within the Python API, you simply exchange calls to genereric compute kernels
over lists of particle pointers with alternative kernel invocations:

1. Delegate the responsibility to use a proper memory pool to the user.
   This can be done via a comment.
2. Ensure that the step you want to vectorise can be based upon continuous
   memory. You can either be sure that this is the case (as you know that
   previous steps have not altered the memory layout), or you can use a
   graph compiler which always inserts explicit sorting steps to ensure
   that all data is continuous.
3. Replace the cell kernel calls with calls to your new bespoke kernels
   that we introduce below.
4. Ensure that the graph compiler uses peano4.toolbox.particles.ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles
   to run through the mesh and build up the local and active sets.


## Wrapping the point to point kernels

Typical Swift 2 kernels look similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  for (auto* localParticle : localParticles) {
    if (
      not localParticle->getCellHasUpdatedParticle()
      and
      marker.isContained( localParticle->getX() )
    ) {
      for (auto* activeParticle : activeParticles) {
        myFancyKernel(localParticle, activeParticle);
      }
    }
  }
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

You loop over the local particles. From the local particles, you pick only
those guys which actually reside within the cell and have not been updated
yet. The rationale behind these ifs are provided @ref page_toolbox_particles_mesh_traversal
"by the generic particle mesh traversal discussion". Note that the
recommendation there says that you should also add a check like
willNotBeSievedIntoNextLevel(). We can omit this one here, as we said
beforehand that we use the optimised kernels if and only if we know that
the particle-mesh association is stationary and consistent.

We now follow the discussion of peano4.toolbox.particles.ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles
and replace this kernel invocation by

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  typename std::list< typename ParticleContainer::value_type >::const_iterator localParticlesIterator  = particlesAssociatedWithLocalVertices.begin();

  for (auto localParticlesChunkSize: numberOfParticlesPerLocalVertex) {
    typename ParticleContainer::value_type  localParticlesChunk = *localParticlesIterator;
    std::advance( localParticlesIterator, localParticlesChunkSize );

    typename std::list< typename ParticleContainer::value_type >::const_iterator activeParticlesIterator = activeParticles.begin();
    for (auto activeParticlesChunkSize: numberOfActiveParticlesPerVertexAdded) {
      typename ParticleContainer::value_type  activeParticlesChunk = *activeParticlesIterator;
      std::advance( activeParticlesIterator, activeParticlesChunkSize );

      for (int localParticleNumberInThisChunk=0; localParticleNumberInThisChunk<localParticlesChunkSize; localParticleNumberInThisChunk++) {
        if (
          marker.isContained(localParticlesChunk[localParticleNumberInThisChunk].getX())
          and
          not localParticlesChunk[localParticleNumberInThisChunk].getCellHasUpdatedParticle()
        ) {
          for (int activeParticleNumberInThisChunk=0; activeParticleNumberInThisChunk<activeParticlesChunkSize; activeParticleNumberInThisChunk++) {
            myFancyKernelVectorised(
              localParticlesChunk  + localParticleNumberInThisChunk,
              activeParticlesChunk + activeParticleNumberInThisChunk
            );
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Note that the force kernel is now replaced by a rewrite that we will discuss
next. You might be able to work without such an overwrite in your particular
case.


## Makeing compiler feedback guide your development

From hereon you can and should ensure that the vectorisation works:
Before we tweak this functions further, I recommend to re-translate with
the Intel compiler, to include -O3 -xHost in the code, and then to manipulate
the Makefile:


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver:  $(CXX_OBJS) $(FORTRAN_OBJS)
	rm observers/StephydroPartForceLoop_sweep62peano4_toolbox_particles_ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles4.o
	$(CXX) -qopt-report=3 $(CXXFLAGS) -c -o observers/StephydroPartForceLoop_sweep62peano4_toolbox_particles_ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles4.o observers/StephydroPartForceLoop_sweep62peano4_toolbox_particles_ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles4.cpp
	$(CXX) $(FORTRAN_MODULE_OBJS) $(FORTRAN_OBJS) $(CXX_OBJS) $(LDFLAGS) $(GPU_OBJS) $(CU_OBJS) $(LIBS) -o noh2D
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where the observer file names have to be replaced with the name of the file
which uses your altered kernel invocation.
This tweaked makefile builds all object files. After that, it deletes the object
file behind the action set StephydroPartForceLoop. This is the step where the
force kernel is actually used. We then retranslate this single object file with
Intel's -opt-report flag which provides ample evidence which vectorisation
optimisation has been performed by the compiler.

You should get a file observers/StephydroPartForceLoop_sweep62peano4_toolbox_particles_ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfListsOverStaticParticles4.optrpt
which summarises if the compiler has vectorised your code and even how much
speedup you might expect. Please note that every rerun of the Python script
overwrites the Makefile. However, there's no need to do this while you tweak
the kernels. Just re-invoke make.

If the optimisation report contains a section like

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
        LOOP BEGIN at ../../../../src/swift2/kernels/ParticleParticleInteraction.cpph (541, 7)

            LOOP BEGIN at ../../../../src/swift2/kernels/ParticleParticleInteraction.cpph (548, 15)
                remark #15553: loop was not vectorized: outer loop is not an auto-vectorization candidate.

                LOOP BEGIN at ../../../../src/swift2/kernels/kernel_hydro.h (148, 3)
                    remark #15344: Loop was not vectorized: vector dependence prevents vectorization
                    remark #15346: vector dependence: assumed FLOW dependence between w (150:19) and w (150:15)
                    remark #25436: Loop completely unrolled by 3
                LOOP END
            LOOP END

            LOOP BEGIN at ../../../../src/swift2/kernels/ParticleParticleInteraction.cpph (556, 15)
            LOOP END
        LOOP END
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

then this is really bad news. Line 556 is the loop that you want to be
vectorised, so the

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
for (int activeParticleNumberInThisChunk=0; activeParticleNumberInThisChunk<activeParticlesChunkSize; activeParticleNumberInThisChunk++) {
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

No vectorisation has happened in this case (and the report actually
tells you in this example that kernel_hydro.h:148 is to blame. Once the
compiler manages to optimise, you will see a detailed breakdown of the
predicted speedup.



\subsection section_new_solver_vectorisation_hints Making the core routine vectorise

We've started to collect a list of todos and no-goes for the vectorisation
below. You might want to weak this in one way or the other and it will be
incomplete. But it is a reasonable starting point:

- You cannot collapse the two for loops that invoke the force kernel. That would lead to a scattered
  iteration space in memory and hence make any vectorisation impossible.
- The active loop has to be the inner one.
- The compiler has to be able to inline. According to https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#always-inline-force-inline
  this is something one should be able to control via the annotation

          [[clang::always_inline]]

  but this led to multiple compiler crashes (again with Intel). To some
  degree this makes sense: The function has to be in the header! I played
  around with ipo, but that did not resolve the fundamental issues. So you
  have to put everything used in the for loops into headers.
- You might have to recursively study all inlined methods and make the
  stuff they use inline as well.
- Big if branches are challenging for the vectoriser. Hence, I compute one
  scalar which is either set to 1.0 if we should update and otherwise 0.0. By
  multiplying all updates with this scalar, we can effectively mask out the
  kernel or keep its action. This works as long as all updates are additive,
  i.e. we take the existing value and add something onto it, though some
  slight tweaking also makes it work with the max and min function.
- The tarch::la macros like smaller() or smallerEquals() might stop the
  vectorisation. In this case, you have to move the implementations
  into the header. We don't move all linear algebra routines into the
  header, as this would blow up the executable size. For this
  particular kernel, we have moved the required routines already.
- It seemed originally that one has to replace the std::pow function
  with a specialised version that takes into account that the power is
  an integer. The C++ standard https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/numeric/math/pow
  is not clear if there is still a specialised version of pow for
  integers. However, all seems to vectorise nicely even if we use
  the power with the Intel 2023.2 compiler.
- Complex statements such as

  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  // Update the signal velocity
  localParticle->setV_sig_AV(std::max(localParticle->getV_sig_AV(), mask * v_sig));
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  break the vectorisation. But once you replace them with statements
  similar to

  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  const double currentAV = localParticle->getV_sig_AV();
  const double biggerAV  = mask * v_sig;
  const double newAV     = biggerAV > currentAV ? biggerAV : currentAV;
  localParticle->setV_sig_AV(newAV);
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  vectorisation seems to work.


  See also notes on vectorisation in @ref page_swift_performance_optimisation.


 */


